"""
Apply independently selectable patches to supported OnionUI MainUI binaries.

Requires Python 3.10 or newer.

IMPORTANT: all patches are enabled by default. Use --exclude PATCH to leave
specific patches out. Using --include PATCH switches to an include-only build.
Exclusions always win.

Core ROM-list features:

  patch-rom-list-rows
      Add configurable ROM/Favourites/Recent row counts, calculated row height,
      correct loading/restoration windows, row-specific theme selection-bar
      lookup with stock fallback, centered clipping for folder, game, and
      favourite icons, optional active-theme `gamelist.iconLeftMargin` control
      (0..300) for the aligned folder/game icon layout, row-count-scaled
      horizontal geometry that keeps stock outer spacing through nine rows,
      then reduces outer padding to a 15-pixel minimum; post-icon spacing begins
      reducing at eight rows and reaches a 5-pixel minimum,
      shoulder-button page movement matching the row count,
      first-presentation preview geometry applied before the child list draw,
      a fixed preview-safe Favorite-marker lane by default, automatic far-right
      Favorite-marker placement for deliberate wide game-icon spacers, whole-renderer
      clipping for fixed Favorite rows, and a presence-only compatibility file at
      /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListDynamicFavPos that restores the original
      dynamic preview/no-preview marker position for normal icons. Persisted-window normalization,
      bounded loading recovery, and a completion-gated same-frame preview refresh
      at the stock cover check are retained. The patch
      repairs stale /appconfigs/romwinidx.json
      geometry when a saved window is wider than the current row count or the
      saved selection lies outside its window. The repair edits the three
      successful-lookup stack integers before any menu virtual is called; menu
      restore, worker, and item-accessor ownership remain stock-owned.
      The Language selector deliberately retains its stock six-row geometry.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListRows

      Recommended sample:
        printf '10\\n' > /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListRows

      Rules:
        missing/unreadable/invalid -> 6 rows
        rows = max(6, min(parsed_integer, 20))
        row_height = floor(360 / rows)

  patch-rom-list-font-size
      Add an optional font-size override for ROM/Favourites/Recent and
      contextual-Search result lists. The established active/previous pointer
      tags remain authoritative; exact DBCachedTextMenu identity is consulted
      only after both old checks miss. Popup context menus, Language, Systems,
      Apps, and unrelated menus continue using the theme font.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListFontSize

      Recommended sample:
        printf '22\\n' > /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListFontSize

      Rules:
        missing/unreadable/invalid/non-positive -> use theme font
        font_size = max(8, min(parsed_integer, 60))

  fix-contextual-search
      Fix only the contextual per-console Search path. Consume the confirming
      A/Space release after successful keyboard confirmation, render titles as
      `Console: Search term`, sort results deterministically with the same
      .romListCaseSensitiveSort setting as game lists, retain match-fragment
      highlighting on unselected rows with bright yellow or contrast purple
      selected from theme text luminance, force keyboard key-cap characters to
      white, and remember a
      game launch from Search across MainUI restart. After such
      a launch, B from the restored results skips the no-longer-useful Search
      keyboard parent and returns directly to the source game list. Without a
      game launch, B retains the stock return-to-keyboard behavior.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListCaseSensitiveSort

  patch-rom-list-title-scroll
      Horizontally scroll the selected long game or folder title after an idle
      delay. Only ROM, Favourites, Recent, and Search-result lists are affected.
      Language and unrelated menus retain stock behavior.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListTitleScroll

      Recommended sample:
        printf '700,120\\n' > /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListTitleScroll

      Accepted formats:
        700,120
        700 120

      Values are idle milliseconds and scroll pixels per second. Marquee X is
      derived from an absolute active-scroll wall-clock epoch, so repeated title
      renderer entries cannot multiply the configured speed and thumbnail/no-
      thumbnail repaint differences do not alter pixels per second. Timing uses
      MainUI's existing gettimeofday and SDL_Delay imports rather than custom
      clock and sleep system-call helpers. The selected title draw leaves a
      persistent marker that is consumed by MainUI's actual preview-window
      draw. MainUI's preview-pane left edge, rather than the centered cover's
      narrower rectangle, defines the normal overflow viewport. When the
      selected row draws ic-favorite-mark.png, its actual left edge is also
      treated as an obstruction boundary. Once scrolling starts, the rendered
      title loops continuously as title + 60 blank pixels + title. The visible
      suffix moves left and the repeated prefix re-enters from the right, with
      at most two clipped blits per frame. A live preview still participates
      in the overflow test so ROM-list marquee activation remains identical to
      the proven behavior, but once scrolling is active the moving title uses
      the full label viewport and therefore travels underneath preview artwork.
      Favorite rows remain bounded by the marker's actual destination x; in dynamic
      compatibility mode a no-preview Favorite marker and its marquee can move
      nearly to the far-right edge together. The selected
      row bypasses MainUI's optional highlighted title split so the complete
      original title is measured and rendered as one surface. Contextual Search
      matches remain highlighted while unselected; the selected row uses its
      normal theme selection color and can marquee-scroll without fragmented
      match coloring. The selected row also bypasses MainUI's stock 64-byte display
      scratch pointer after preserving the stock copy, so longer titles scroll
      from the original std::string value. While an overflowing title is armed,
      the patch wraps only the
      central SDL_WaitEvent call and targets a harmless repaint every 33 ms
      (~30 Hz). SDL_Delay sleeps only the remaining time until the next target.
      On the tested MainUI-354 hardware the complete MainUI draw/present loop
      practically lands near 50 ms (~20 Hz); the optimized preview compositor
      and synthetic-frame update gate keep thumbnail and no-thumbnail marquee
      frames in that same cadence bucket. MainUI still performs drawing through
      its normal loop.
      A and B leave-screen transitions preserve the published marquee phase on
      the outgoing row while stopping synthetic repaint wakes, avoiding the
      brief home-position snap on the hardware-tested paths. LEFT remains an
      explicit immediate reset. RIGHT/Game Details can still redraw the
      outgoing row once at its home position; this cosmetic limitation is
      documented and currently left unfixed.
      Missing or unreadable files disable scrolling. A negative idle value or a
      non-positive speed also disables scrolling.
      Enabled values are clamped:
        idle:   10..30000 ms (0..9 clamp to 10)
        speed:  5..400 pixels/second

  patch-rom-list-letter-jump
      Use R1 and L1 to jump to the next or previous initial-character group in
      ROM, Favourites, Recent, and Search-result lists. Each accepted jump plays
      MainUI's normal navigation click once; held repeats are silent while an
      asynchronous scan is pending. R2/L2 page movement and all shoulder-button
      behavior in non-game menus remain stock. Grouping uses the first non-space
      UTF-8 code point, with ASCII and Latin-1 uppercase folded to lowercase.
      All game lists use the contiguous displayed-initial scan. During Refresh
      Roms, short-name Arcade systems bind the resolved GetGameName label and
      matching byte length to SQLite's disp field, so database order matches the
      labels MainUI draws. Ordinary systems retain stock cache fields.
      Asynchronous scans use one stock loader window at a time and keep the
      visible selection fixed while scanning. Pending progress survives unrelated
      actions, resumes on completion/idle events or another L1/R1 press, and is
      discarded if the visible selection changes. Once the target is known, the
      helper publishes it, queues the direction-matched final stock loader, and
      posts one ordinary repaint for already-resident destinations.

  patch-rom-list-end-jump
      Add direction-modified shoulder navigation in ROM, Favourites, Recent,
      and Search-result lists. Hold UP before pressing L2 to jump to the first
      item; hold DOWN before pressing R2 to jump to the final item. L1/R1 letter
      jumping is unchanged. L2/R2 retain stock page movement when the required
      direction was not already held. An accepted modified shoulder is consumed
      and latched until that shoulder is released. Stock MainUI has no dedicated
      Home/End game-list action.

  show-gamelist-metadata-in-game-details
      Read genre, rating and description from the selected ROM's immediate
      gamelist.xml first. For ROMs below the standard /Roms/<system>/ root,
      fall back to that system-root gamelist.xml using the ROM-relative path.
      MainUI's stock miyoogamelist.xml database-generation path remains separate
      and unchanged by this metadata feature. Bounded path-to-offset indexes are built lazily
      and retained in a sixteen-entry, full-XML-path LRU cache with a 2 MiB
      dynamic index-memory budget and a 256 KiB per-index ceiling. XML size and mtime are checked when a new
      detail lookup opens; missing paths are negatively cached but still statted.
      Genre and a 0-10 rating share one compact line below the title; the
      description uses a normal-style font at 75% of the detail system-name font size (minimum 15 px).
      L2/R2 scroll long descriptions by one visible page. No metadata is written
      to cache6.db.

  skip-folders-in-game-detail-navigation
      Skip folder rows when Up/Down changes the selected game inside the detail
      view, in both ordinary ROM lists and Favourites. List and detail counters
      use game-only ordinals. Favourite and ordinary ROM-folder rows suppress
      the normal bottom-right N/N counter.

  patch-theme-list-font-bold
      Read an optional boolean `gamelist.bold` member from the active theme's
      config.json and apply it to game-list row fonts. Missing, invalid, or true
      values preserve stock bold; false selects a private normal-style game-list
      font so shared MainUI fonts are never restyled. Language, Systems, Apps,
      Settings, titles, details, and other non-game fonts are not changed. Theme
      scan results are staged by exact path and only the selected active theme
      may populate live patched theme state. This also styles the optional
      ROM-list size-override font when both patches are enabled.

  fix-game-list-rapid-navigation
      Reset MainUI's built-in rapid-navigation history when the translated
      action changes. Stock MainUI leaves a long shoulder or direction history
      active when another action arrives within 200 ms, so a single ordinary
      Up/Down action can incorrectly select accelerated movement. Consecutive
      repeats of the same action retain stock acceleration. With
      patch-rom-list-rows enabled, that acceleration uses the effective visible
      row count; otherwise it retains the stock six-entry distance.

  limit-rom-list-thumbnail-cache
      Limit MainUI's decoded game-preview LRU cache to 64 surfaces instead of
      the stock effective maximum of 1001. The patch recognizes only cache
      instances using the game-preview SDL_FreeSurface cleanup callback, then
      leaves MainUI's existing mutex, least-recently-used eviction order, and
      surface cleanup path intact. Other generic MainUI caches retain their
      stock 1001-entry ceiling.

  skip-rom-preview-surface-copy
      Bypass MainUI's redundant SDL_ConvertSurface(surface, surface->format, 0)
      clone in both the shared 250x360 game-preview loader and the four stock
      theme/skin image-loading paths. All five sites call IMG_Load directly.
      Preview-specific 32-bit normalization and scaling remain stock, while theme
      surfaces keep IMG_Load's original format just as they did before the
      no-op self-format clone. This removes one full-surface allocation and pixel
      copy per preview load and for roughly eighty theme assets during startup.

  cache-list-skin-images
      Cache the three skin-surface families that MainUI otherwise decodes again
      for every newly constructed list window: selection backgrounds
      (skin/bg-list-s.png or the active bg-list-s_N.png),
      skin/ic-favorite-mark.png, and skin/icon-TF.png.  The cache is deliberately
      limited to these constructor-owned assets; unrelated IMG_Load callers keep
      their stock ownership.  Cached surfaces live for the MainUI process and
      each constructor receives its own SDL_ConvertSurface clone, so stock
      destructor/free and runtime-reload ownership stays unchanged.  If the active-theme path changes at
      runtime, new loads bypass the cache rather than risking stale cross-theme
      sharing; normal Onion ThemeSwitcher restarts MainUI and therefore starts a
      fresh cache.

  skip-identity-thumbnail-rescale
      Reuse an already normalized 32-bit preview surface when the requested
      preview width exactly matches the decoded source width.  Stock already
      reuses the surface when the request is larger; equality unnecessarily
      allocates another same-size surface and runs bitmap_scale.  The downscale
      path derives height from the source aspect ratio, so equal width also
      implies equal output height.  Real downscales remain unchanged.

  optimize-favourite-refresh-sweep
      Avoid walking every Favourite TextItem on every mode-2 update solely to
      test its deferred-delete byte.  MainUI has exactly one runtime setter for
      that byte; this patch marks a tiny dirty flag there and runs the unchanged
      stock destruction/erase sweep only on the next mode-2 update after such a
      request.  Ordinary updates return the same zero/no-removal result without
      traversing the list.  The pre-preview root-restore hook remains first.

  optimize-direct-shell-operations
      Replace trivial startup and launch system() calls with direct imported
      open/write/close/unlink operations. This covers the startup governor write,
      /tmp/stay_awake creation, both /tmp/no_audioserver creation/removal launch
      variants, and removes the redundant __xstat existence probe immediately
      before an already-null-checked fopen during system-directory scanning.
      When the burst-governor patch is excluded, command semantics remain stock
      and the startup governor is still written as ondemand. When both are
      selected, the burst patch supersedes only that startup governor write.

  skip-rom-artwork-directory-scan
      Exclude the root-level Onion artwork directory named Imgs from both cache
      rebuild tree walks: the pre-count pass that feeds the "Scanning..." total
      and the real DB-building pass. The guard runs before child-path construction
      and recursion, so neither pass enters/enumerates Imgs. Ordinary subfolders,
      including any deeper same-named folder, retain stock recursion behavior.

  throttle-background-status-polling
      Keep the stock one-second hotplug-thread wake cadence while running the
      battery and Wi-Fi probes together on the first tick and then every fifth
      tick. A cheap in-memory Wi-Fi-enabled check returns the disabled state
      without walking /proc, and the same guard is moved before the startup
      wpa_supplicant scan. Other one-second hotplug work remains stock.

  optimize-burst-cpu-governor
      Use one nested, reference-counted performance-governor burst across MainUI
      startup, both ROM-cache rebuild functions, and both verified game/app launch
      handoff variants. The current governor is saved, performance is written by
      direct syscalls, and the saved token is restored after startup/rebuild work
      and before normal or fatal MainUI exit. Launch bursts therefore cover MainUI
      preparation/teardown but do not remain active throughout gameplay. A small
      /tmp recovery marker is retained only after interruption or failed restore;
      the next MainUI startup retries it. Failed marker/sysfs operations fail open.

  optimize-rom-database-deletion
      Collapse the thirteen normal per-system ROM-cache shell deletions to:
        rm -f /mnt/SDCARD/Roms/*/*_cache*
      MainUI's distinct fallback command, `rm -rf /mnt/P2EXT/.dbs/*`, remains
      stock so a cold miyoogamelist database rebuild cannot reuse stale derived
      DB state. Refresh gating, logging, cleanup, and final sync() remain stock.

  optimize-rom-database-rebuild
      Bulk-load the two derived cache6.db rebuild paths with:
        PRAGMA synchronous=OFF;
        PRAGMA journal_mode=OFF;
        PRAGMA temp_store=MEMORY;
        PRAGMA cache_size=-8000;
        BEGIN TRANSACTION;
      When optimize-rom-list-database-indexes is also selected, create the
      runtime-selected BINARY/NOCASE browse index after all INSERTs and before
      commit. Index creation and commit remain separate so a failed index build
      still commits a usable unindexed derived cache. locking_mode=EXCLUSIVE is
      deliberately omitted because its result shape and connection lifetime have
      not been proven safe. After an interrupted rebuild, run Refresh Roms again.


  fix-rom-rebuild-path-safety
      Harden the recursive cache6.db directory scan against long filesystem paths
      and low-memory allocation failures. The shared path buffer is 4096 bytes,
      all three stock sprintf path builders are replaced by bounded snprintf
      wrappers that reject truncation, the 128-byte filename scratch is always
      terminated after strncpy, and both rebuild malloc results are checked before
      use. Overlong entries fail locally instead of writing past a buffer.

  fix-wifi-network-shell-quoting
      Build the three wpa_cli connect commands with shell-safe single-quoted
      arguments before calling system(). Spaces, $, backticks, semicolons and
      other shell metacharacters remain literal SSID/passphrase bytes; embedded
      apostrophes are escaped with the standard close/escape/reopen sequence.
      Scanning is unchanged; only the connect-time SSID/PSK commands are patched.

  optimize-miyoogamelist-file-checks
      Build one bounded transient filename set before importing miyoogamelist.xml
      and use it for ordinary root-level ROM existence checks. Allocation,
      capacity, non-ASCII, nested-path, and unusual-path cases fall back to the
      stock per-entry access() call.

  suppress-miyoogamelist-entry-timing
      Skip the two gettimeofday calls inside the miyoogamelist.xml per-entry
      loop. The two calls bracketing the whole import remain, preserving the
      existing overall timing measurement.

  cache-favourite-lookups-per-page
      Without Favourite folders, read favourite.json once into a bounded
      page-local buffer and reuse MainUI's matcher for each ROM row. When
      add-favourite-folders-json is active, its persistent exact-path hash is
      used instead and this page cache is automatically superseded. Relative
      Arcade rompaths are hashed exactly rather than falling into label scans.

  add-favourite-folders-json
      Keep stock favourite.json flat while a durable favourite-folders.json
      sidecar stores immutable folder IDs, hierarchy, order and stable item
      assignments. A restored same-boot root selection queues one ordinary
      repaint event only when the draw-time correction changes the selected row,
      so stock thumbnail loading immediately observes the restored game without
      synthetic navigation. No legacy folder/fpath migration is performed. A executes
      the complete stock FolderWindow constructor/load/push transaction
      synchronously; B uses the stock close lifecycle. Select places Move selected
      or Move here first, followed by Create folder; Sort A-Z is available only for
      selected games, not folders. Rename and Delete remain folder actions while X
      remains Search. The cut marker refreshes immediately. Move here places the
      cut folder or item at the selected row in the current directory, including
      downward moves and the final row, while retaining the required
      folders-before-ROMs grouping. Enter another folder before pasting to move
      into it. Normal ROM SELECT dispatches stock Add/Remove Favorite labels and
      action constructors from fresh exact-path membership. Custom Favorite-list
      deletion is isolated to the copied Favorite TextMenu vtable; the ordinary
      stock DeleteRomAction dispatcher is not globally intercepted.

  sort-favourite-folder-a-z
      Add Sort A-Z as the third Favourite SELECT action for selected games, after
      Move selected or Move here and Create folder, when the folder sidecar patch
      is active. Hide it when the selected row is a folder. Sort only the current
      folder, retain folders before ordinary rows, persist both groups through the atomic sidecar writer, and clear a
      pending cut identity after a successful reorder. Translation ID 406 is
      private to this patch.

  optimize-recent-list-loading
      Build Recent game rows directly from the label already stored in
      recentlist.json. Bypass both stock GetGameName calls, which otherwise
      trigger an expensive one-time Arcade-name initialization on the first
      Recent open. App lookup and launch metadata handling remain stock.

  fix-recent-preview-paths
      Keep Recent game identity and thumbnail resolution consistent across direct
      console and Onion Search launches. Search-origin `launch.sh:/real/rom`
      records normalize to the source emulator + real ROM before Recent duplicate
      comparison/insertion, exact `launch == "setstate"` pseudo-records are
      filtered before they consume retained slots, and list/initial Details/
      Up-Down Details use one explicit-PNG-or-source-image-directory rule.

  fix-search-favourite-status
      Keep Favorite membership consistent for games shown through Onion's synthetic
      Search console. Search database rows encode the source launcher and real
      ROM path as `launch.sh:/real/rom`; normalize that Search-only identity for
      star checks, context-menu Add/Remove decisions, removal, and newly stored
      Favorite records. New adds keep the stock Favorite schema but store the
      source emulator launcher plus real ROM path.

  use-rom-database-display-names
      Treat SQLite cache6.db `disp` as the authoritative visible ROM title while
      browsing an existing database. Skip MainUI's legacy runtime GetGameName
      pass even for systems marked short-name/Arcade. GetGameName remains
      available to database construction/rebuild paths only.

  extend-recent-list-to-50
      Inspect at most the first 200 successfully parsed records from the top of
      recentlist.json while retaining at most 50 rows. During file reconstruction
      only, the first/top occurrence of a duplicate Recent identity wins and later
      unique records cannot displace the first 50 retained rows. Live launches keep
      stock move-to-front duplicate behavior.

  exclude-apps-from-recent-list
      Skip the verified live AppAction Add2RecentList call and stale type-3
      AppAction rows during recentlist.json reconstruction while leaving the
      separate game-launch call unchanged. Apps, Themes, Tweaks and custom AppAction
      launches no longer create new Recent records. Existing App records are not
      removed. When the burst-governor selector is active, an acquire-only
      wrapper preserves app-launch performance without writing to Recent.

  add-recent-remove-from-list
      Insert translation ID 52, Remove from list, as the second Recent SELECT
      menu row, immediately below Start. Game rows retain MainUI's exact stock
      erase-key path. App and other non-game rows snapshot their launch path or
      visible saved label while the row is alive, locate the matching bounded
      Recent record without assuming a stored record type, and erase that record
      through MainUI's stock iterator and writer. Then remove only the selected
      visible TextMenu row through the stock single-item method. The popup uses
      the matching three-row background.

  restore-favourite-folder-after-game-exit
      Preserve stock root-Favourites return behavior and add a one-shot /tmp
      record when a game is launched from a Favourite folder. Root Favorites
      viewport state also has a tiny /tmp same-boot fallback so it survives a
      MainUI restart caused by launching a game elsewhere. On the next
      Favourites load, construct root normally, rebuild the real nested folder
      window stack one level at a time, and restore the launched row only after
      the target window is active. Queue exactly one SDL wake event per folder
      level plus final row selection. At Favourite root, B is handled explicitly
      as close/quit instead of being delegated to the stock list dispatcher.
      Intermediate return-chain frames are not drawn; the first visible frame is
      the completed destination. The implementation retains the physically tested
      Favourite core and applies three verified branch changes: two around its inlined
      root cleanup and one at the Favourite-root B path. The record is consumed
      immediately and does not survive a reboot.

  add-parent-folder-to-rom-lists
      Add a leading ``..`` folder row to every non-root ordinary ROM directory.
      Selecting it returns to the parent exactly like pressing B once. Root and
      search lists are unchanged. Favourite ``..`` rows are part of
      add-favourite-folders-json.


  suppress-rom-database-debug-logging
      Suppress sixteen unconditional development/debug output calls: one in Recent loading, four in
      preview image processing, three in ROM metadata/cache loading, three in
      each database page fetch, two in the directory-scan rebuild loop, one
      per-item "refresh %s removed %d" call that Favourite-mode windows
      (including folder views) execute for every list entry on every
      window-update pass, plus TextMenu::freeListItem's per-row and final
      teardown lines. Also skip five gettimeofday calls used only by the removed
      preview/page timing messages. The Favourite helper's pending-deletion
      sweep and all TextMenu destruction/free operations are kept; error
      diagnostics plus one-time menu and rebuild summary logging remain intact.

  skip-rom-database-refresh-sync
      Skip three process-wide sync() calls associated with derived ROM caches:
      one after bulk cache-file deletion and one after each of the XML-import and
      directory-scan cache6.db rebuild transactions. A power loss can require
      Refresh Roms again, but ROMs, gamelist.xml, favourites, and settings are
      not modified by this patch.

  cache-rom-list-counts
      Cache repeated ROM-list COUNT(*) results in RAM, keyed by system object and
      exact generated SQL, with generation invalidation after rebuild/delete
      mutations. Cache hits stay on the stock success continuation;
      only real COUNT queries call sqlite3_reset() to release their read lock
      without destroying MainUI's prepared statement object.

  reuse-onion-arcade-name-lookup
      For short-name arcade-style systems, replace Onion's allocation-heavy
      std::map initialization/lookup path during database rebuilds with one
      process-local packed hash cache loaded from arcade-rom-names.txt, then
      reuse the first resolved name and pinyin result for both MainUI language
      slots. Any cache-initialization failure falls back to stock GetGameName.
      Requires OnionOS 4.2.2 or newer and its file-backed libgamename.so.
      Enabled by default; exclude it for the stock Miyoo library or another
      replacement that does not use Onion's arcade name file semantics.

  optimize-rom-list-database-indexes
      When MainUI rebuilds a per-system cache6.db, create a browse index matching
      WHERE ppath=? ORDER BY type DESC, disp. When runtime-selectable sorting is
      also patched, the same cached .romListCaseSensitiveSort marker selects the
      refresh schema: an absent or unreadable marker creates COLLATE NOCASE, while
      an existing readable marker creates the stock BINARY index. Existing cache databases need one Refresh Roms after
      installing the patched MainUI. Refreshes then recreate the matching index
      automatically.

Optional MainUI fixes:

  patch-key-repeat-settings
      Read MainUI's global SDL key-repeat delay and interval from the SD card
      once during startup. This changes held-button repeat speed throughout
      MainUI, including game lists, Systems, Apps, Settings, and detail views.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.mainUIKeyRepeat

      Quicker-repeat examples:
        300,50
        300 50

      Default-behavior examples:
        500,100
        500 100

      The first value is the initial delay in milliseconds; the second is the
      interval between repeats. Missing or unreadable files, and non-positive
      parsed values, use default MainUI values: 500 ms delay and 100 ms interval.
      Input must use a comma- or space-separated form above without leading whitespace.
      Parsed values are clamped to these conservative ranges:
        delay:    100..2000 ms
        interval:  30..500 ms

  fix-game-detail-title-on-open
      Correct the initial GameDetailWindow title/label argument order so the
      full display title appears immediately after pressing Right.

  fix-sleep-timer-left-right
      Make Settings > Sleep Timer Left cycle opposite to Right.

  patch-main-menu-layout
      Replace the stock type-0 main-menu builder and SELECT popup with a
      read-only, bounded configuration loaded once per MainUI session from:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/main-menu.json

      The `menu` object accepts recents, favorites, games, apps, settings and
      expert booleans. Only literal true enables a key, and recognized object-key
      order is the visible main-menu order. One to four visible sections use
      their actual count; larger menus retain stock four-column paging. Only
      the six top-level main-menu row labels are eligible for wrapping. Those
      labels are measured with the active font inside a centered maximum
      156-pixel title area with 10-pixel safety insets and wrap to at most two
      UTF-8-safe lines. Every other menu label follows the stock renderer
      unchanged. Two-line labels retain full glyph-height draw rectangles, but
      their vertical centres are separated by 75% of the measured font-derived
      height to avoid the unused SDL_ttf ascender/descender area looking double-spaced. Missing, unreadable, malformed, empty or all-false
      menu definitions fall back to Favorite, Games, Apps and Settings. Existing
      readable .showRecents and .showExpert markers are compatibility
      fallbacks only. A marker enables its section when the corresponding JSON
      key is omitted; an explicit true or false value in `menu` takes precedence.
      A marker-enabled omitted section is appended after the declared order when
      that order remains active; fallback menus retain stock order.

      The `settings` object accepts shutdown, brightness, wifi, display,
      themes, tweaks, language, sound, sleep and about. Object form starts
      from the current Settings defaults: omitted keys remain enabled, explicit
      false hides only that row, and explicitly mentioned keys take precedence
      in the visible order while omitted rows follow in the normal order. An
      array remains an explicit enabled-row allowlist. Capability checks still
      suppress unavailable Wi-Fi/language/Theme/Tweaks rows. Settings is never
      allowed to resolve to zero usable rows; an empty result restores the safe
      current defaults instead of constructing an empty crash-prone menu.

      The `context` object accepts search, refresh, recents,
      favorites, games, apps, settings, expert, themes, tweaks and
      custom1/custom2/custom3. Its
      key order is the popup order; false entries remain registered but hidden.
      A permissive array of names is also accepted. Missing or malformed JSON,
      and a valid root object without a `context` property, default to Refresh
      all roms, Search and Tweaks when their external launchers are available.
      When `context` is explicitly present, omitted
      keys are not invented; `search` is therefore off unless enabled. Root
      `hotkey` defaults true; set it to
      false to disable L1+R1+L2+R2. When enabled, the chord reveals all
      registered false context entries for that MainUI session and opens the
      popup. Other missing keys are not invented.
      Every numbered PopupWindow request for bg-pop-menu-1.png through
      bg-pop-menu-6.png is resolved through the active theme. The viewport is
      capped at six rows; if an exact numbered background is absent, the highest
      readable smaller one is expanded with preserved borders and repeated
      fixed 60-pixel row bodies to the requested height. Additional actions
      scroll.

      Optional `search` uses MainUI's stock AppAction handoff to
      /mnt/SDCARD/App/Search/launch.sh with translation ID 153. Section shortcuts use MainUI's
      stock CallbackAction class, the same ABI used by Refresh all roms. The callback queues the requested stock section,
      posts one harmless wake event, and returns the popup's normal success
      result. Immediately after MainUI completes the popup-release event, the
      patch reproduces the stock top-level transaction: allocate the section
      window, call MainUI's string-title constructor with the translated title
      and real section target, activate it, push it on the stock window stack,
      and return zero. The patch intentionally does not alter stock boot-direct selection.
      Theme Switcher and Tweaks use fixed type-3 launch paths. The selector also
      restores MainUI's stock-hidden Settings Themes row and the adjacent hidden
      Fixes row (shown as Tweaks) when their respective external launchers exist.
      Those Settings rows dispatch through the real stock destination handlers
      and propagate the AppAction result immediately; the stock theme-directory
      startup scan remains untouched. Up to three
      custom entries provide an untranslated label, launch path and integer
      type; both label and launch are required. The parser accepts comments,
      trailing commas, unknown keys and bounded escaped strings, but never
      writes or normalizes the file. Built-in context labels fall back to
      embedded English when translation output is missing, empty, or exactly one
      space; top-level main-menu labels remain untouched. Private language ID
      407 is used for Tweaks, with the same fallback rule.

  fix-invalid-main-menu-state
      Fall back to localized string ID 2 (Game) for invalid menu/translation
      IDs instead of displaying MainUI's internal wrongbeef fallback.

  fix-game-list-context-menu-background
      Use the three-row popup background for the ordinary three-item game-list
      context menu instead of the two-row popup background.

  fix-dialog-action-theme-style
      Render generic and confirmation-dialog action labels such as OK and
      Cancel with the theme's hint font and hint color. This includes the
      separate Settings > Shutdown confirmation renderer.

  fix-case-sensitive-game-list-sorting
      Add runtime-selectable game-list sorting. By default, normal and searched
      ROM lists use SQLite COLLATE NOCASE so uppercase and lowercase titles are
      sorted together. Creating the marker file restores stock case-sensitive
      ordering. Its contents are ignored, so it may be zero bytes.

      Runtime configuration:
        /mnt/SDCARD/.tmp_update/config/.romListCaseSensitiveSort

      Rules:
        absent/unreadable -> case-insensitive sorting
        exists/readable   -> stock case-sensitive sorting

  fix-game-detail-title-wrap-width
      Replace MainUI's character-count title wrapping with pixel-measured,
      font-aware UTF-8 wrapping. Each line is measured using the actual detail
      title font and is limited to 340 pixels inside the existing 350-pixel
      drawing rectangle, leaving a 10-pixel safety margin. Wrapping prefers
      spaces and falls back to UTF-8 code-point boundaries for oversized words.
      Hard wraps do not append an artificial hyphen, and the final title
      character is preserved.

  improve-game-details
      Improve the complete game-detail layout. Draw the stored system/emulator
      label at x=274,y=98,w=360, move the title and metadata base to y=133,
      align the counter's first visible digit at x=276,y=55, and place the
      favourite icon at a clamped 10-pixel top-right margin. The redundant
      "Added to favorites" text is skipped. The right-side text rectangle becomes
      x=274,w=360, and both real and default previews retain stock centering but
      are shifted 70 pixels upward. This option also enables
      fix-game-detail-title-on-open.

  prevent-favourite-label-collisions
      Refuse to append a Favourite whose exact quoted display label already
      exists. During load, duplicate labels already present on disk are skipped
      without enabling MainUI's destructive stock rewrite routine. The file is
      left byte-for-byte unchanged; already accumulated/corrupted files still
      require an explicit repair tool.

  escape-apostrophes-in-rom-paths
      Double apostrophes before ROM/folder paths are interpolated into MainUI's
      SQL queries. The original C++ strings are not modified.

Patch selection:

  --exclude PATCH
      Start with every patch enabled and remove PATCH. Repeat the option or use
      comma-separated names to remove several patches.

  --include PATCH
      Switch to an include-only build and add PATCH. Repeat the option or use
      comma-separated names.

  --patch-all
      Explicitly select every patch. This is optional because all patches are
      already selected when no include selector is supplied. --exclude still
      removes patches from an explicit --patch-all build.

  Patch names are listed below and are passed to --include or --exclude.

Examples:

  # Show help.
  python3 onionos_mainui_patcher.py --help

  # Apply every available patch (the default).
  python3 onionos_mainui_patcher.py MainUI-354-clean

  # Apply everything except title scrolling and the sync tradeoff.
  python3 onionos_mainui_patcher.py \
    --exclude patch-rom-list-title-scroll \
    --exclude skip-rom-database-refresh-sync \
    MainUI-354-clean

  # Apply only dynamic rows and the game-list font override.
  python3 onionos_mainui_patcher.py \
    --include patch-rom-list-rows,patch-rom-list-font-size \
    MainUI-354-clean


By default the output is FILE.patched. With --inplace, the input is replaced
and FILE.pre-mainui-patches.bak is created first if it does not already exist.
Known repository MainUI binaries are recommended, but an unrecognized SHA-256 is
accepted with a prominent compatibility warning. ELF, protected-data, and selected
hook signatures are still verified before output is written.

Requires Python 3.10 or newer.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import hashlib
import os
import re
import shutil
import struct
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path

